    "created_at": "2018-10-26T17:50:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-10-26T17:50:52Z",
    "body": "> Given that meta http-equiv=set-cookie can be dynamically constructed and inserted into the HEAD by script at any time (and modifies the cookie jar when added to the document), my intuition is that it should be restricted similarly to document.cookie in cases where scripting is allowed, but this leaves the question of whether to allow it when scripting is not allowed -- whether by UI setting, by iframe sandboxing, or partially disallowed by content security policy forbidding some or all inline scripts.\r\n\r\nYes. We're using CSP to block scripts but relied on this to set cookies in static pages. For the life of me, I can't understand why anyone thought the ability for a cookie to be set using simple HTML needed to be killed after all these years.",
